SEVERE A Step-by-Step Speech Practice Guide For people who experience severe stammering and want noticeable improvement. How to use this guide Practice calmly and consistently. The aim is not to force words out or speak unnaturally slowly. Use the exercises to develop relaxed breathing, controlled speech, comfortable vowel sounds, body awareness, and confidence in everyday conversations. This is a 1.5-year program with once-daily practice. TIMING & COMMITMENT: Practice schedule: Once daily • Session: 20-25 minutes at a time of day that works best for you • Morning recommended for fresh mind and positive tone setting • Sets foundation for daily interactions • With consistent practice, minimal interruptions needed Duration: 1.5 years of consistent practice (once daily) • Months 1-3: Exercises feel new, breathing becomes automatic • Months 4-6: Vocal exercises strengthen, body awareness develops • Months 7-9: Real-world improvements show, confidence builds • Months 10-15: Real-world fluency increases, anxiety decreases • Months 16-18: Changes feel permanent, stammering substantially reduced The 1.5-year commitment with consistent once-daily practice is essential. Extended practice allows results to develop naturally and sustainably for severe stammering. By month 6, you will notice significant improvements. By month 18, stammering will be substantially reduced (50-70%). Important: This is an educational self-practice guide, not a medical treatment or guaranteed cure. If stammering is persistent, worsening, causing significant distress, or substantially interfering with school, work, or relationships, consider working with a qualified speech-language pathologist/speech therapist.Daily sequence — ONCE DAILY PRACTICE Practice one session daily for 20-25 minutes 1. Breathing (4 min) 20 slow nose breaths + 11 alternating-nostril repetitions 2. Vocal exercises (10 min) 20 words for each vowel sound, with 5–7 second vowel stretching 3. Mirror practice (5 min) Speak slowly in front of mirror. Observe body movements. Identify tension in jaw, shoulders, neck. Practice relaxation. 4. Slow reading (5 min) Read 4–5 words per comfortable mouth-inhale, using gentle vowel stretching 5. Subconscious rewiring (1–2 min) Focus on relaxation. Say: 'My voice is calm. My body is relaxed. I speak with confidence.' DURATION: Continue once daily for 1.5 years minimum • Consistency matters more than perfection • 20-25 minutes per day • Skip a session? Resume the next day. Do not skip two in a row. • Stick with it. Changes are significant and permanent after 1.5 years.STEP 1 — BREATHING EXERCISES The first goal is to make your breathing slow, steady, and relaxed before you begin speaking. A. Slow nasal breathing — 20 repetitions 1. Sit or stand comfortably and relax your shoulders. 2. Inhale slowly through your nose. 3. Exhale slowly through your nose. 4. Keep the breath comfortable. Do not force a deep breath. 5. Repeat 20 times. Practice cue: slow in → slow out → relaxed body. B. Alternating-nostril breathing — 11 repetitions 1. Close your left nostril gently with your finger. 2. Inhale slowly through your right nostril. 3. Release the left nostril and gently close the right nostril. 4. Exhale slowly through the left nostril. 5. Continue the pattern for 11 repetitions. 6. Keep the breathing comfortable rather than trying to take very large breaths. If you feel dizzy, uncomfortable, or short of breath, stop and return to normal breathing.STEP 2 — VOCAL EXERCISES Take a comfortable breath through your mouth, then gently stretch the target vowel for about 5–7 seconds before completing the word. Example: apple → aaaaaaaapple Do not squeeze your throat or push the voice. The stretch should feel gentle and controlled. Vowel A — 20 words aaaaaaapple, aaaaaaanimal, aaaaaaanswer, aaaaaaartist, aaaaaaafternoon, aaaaaaairport, aaaaaaactor, aaaaaaaddress, aaaaaaadventure, aaaaaaangel, aaaaaaapron, aaaaaaanchor, aaaaaaavocado, aaaaaaarrow, aaaaaaautumn, aaaaaaaamaze, aaaaaaaarena, aaaaaaapace, aaaaaaasafe, aaaaaaataste Vowel E — 20 words eeeeeeegg, eeeeeeeleephant, eeeeeeenergy, eeeeeeengine, eeeeeeevening, eeeeeeeagle, eeeeeeearth, eeeeeeecho, eeeeeeelbow, eeeeeeeenvelope, eeeeeeexercise, eeeeeeentry, eeeeeeevent, eeeeeeexample, eeeeeeelevator, eeeeeeeend, eeeeeeeelt, eeeeeeeable, eeeeeeeave, eeeeeeeaze Vowel I — 20 words iiiiiiice, iiiiiiidea, iiiiiiisland, iiiiiiimage, iiiiiiinside, iiiiiiinsect, iiiiiiinvite, iiiiiiimportant, iiiiiiinterest, iiiiiiinternet, iiiiiiinstant, iiiiiiincome, iiiiiiinjury, iiiiiiitem, iiiiiiimagine, iiiiiiiink, iiiiiiinvest, iiiiiiiion, iiiiiiiill, iiiiiiiince Vowel O — 20 words ooooooorange, ooooooocean, ooooooopen, oooooooffice, ooooooorder, ooooooonly, ooooooowner, oooooooasis, ooooooobject, oooooooutside, ooooooovertime, oooooooption, oooooooval, ooooooonion, oooooooriginal, ooooooone, oooooooven, oooooooose, oooooooold, oooooooose Vowel U — 20 words uuuuuuumbrella, uuuuuuuncle, uuuuuuunder, uuuuuuunit, uuuuuuuse, uuuuuuusual, uuuuuuuniverse, uuuuuuunique, uuuuuuuniform, uuuuuuup, uuuuuuupper, uuuuuuurgent, uuuuuuuseful, uuuuuuunderstand, uuuuuuupdated, uuuuuuugh, uuuuuuudge, uuuuuuugly, uuuuuuultra, uuuuuuuponSTEP 3 — MIRROR PRACTICE & BODY AWARENESS Stammering is not just about speech. It often involves tension in your body—jaw clenching, shoulder shrugging, neck tension, eye blinking patterns, head movements. Mirror practice helps you identify and eliminate these unwanted physical responses. Why mirror practice matters When you speak, you typically cannot see your own body. Mirror practice gives you real-time feedback on physical tension. As you notice these movements, you can consciously relax and eliminate them. This breaks the full-body stammer pattern—not just the voice stammer. How to practice (5 min daily) Setup: Stand in front of a mirror. Ensure good lighting so you can see your face and shoulders clearly. Step 1: Observe (1 min) 1. Look at yourself calmly in the mirror 2. Notice your baseline: Do you see tension? 3. Identify problem areas:- Jaw tension? (clenched teeth, tight jaw)- Shoulder tension? (shrugged shoulders, tightness)- Neck tension? (stiff neck, visible straining)- Facial tension? (furrowed brow, tight lips)- Eye movements? (excessive blinking, averted gaze)- Head movements? (head tilts, jerky motions) Step 2: Practice with awareness (3 min) 1. Speak slowly: Say your name, then a sentence: 'My name is [name]. I speak with calm and confidence.' 2. Watch yourself: As you speak, observe your body 3. Identify the moment tension appears: When do you clench? When do you tense? 4. Make note: Write down which body part shows most tension Step 3: Relax & eliminate (1 min) 1. Consciously relax the tense area: Relax jaw, drop shoulders, soften neck 2. Speak the same sentence again: 'My name is [name]. I speak with calm and confidence.' 3. Observe the difference: Is there less tension? Is speech smoother? 4. Practice 3 times: Each time, focus on keeping that body part relaxed What you're training• Awareness: Knowing when & where you tense up • Control: Consciously relaxing tense areas • Confidence: Speaking while relaxed feels better • Habit: After 1.5 years, relaxed speech becomes automatic Mirror practice checklist Each session, check off: Identified tense areas Consciously relaxed them Noticed improvement in fluency Wrote down which body part improved mostSTEP 4 — SLOW READING PRACTICE Now transfer the controlled breathing and vocal practice into connected speech. How to practice 1. Choose a short paragraph, story, article, or book. 2. Take a comfortable inhale through your mouth. 3. Read approximately 4–5 words on that breath. 4. Use the same gentle vowel-stretching technique while saying the words, aiming for approximately 5–6 seconds on the stretched vowel when practicing. 5. Pause, breathe comfortably, and continue. 6. Focus on smoothness and relaxation rather than speed. 7. If practicing in front of mirror, notice body tension and maintain relaxation Example rhythm Breathe → relax body → read 4–5 words slowly → pause → breathe → read the next 4–5 words. Important: The purpose is controlled practice, not permanently speaking every word with a long 5–6 second stretch. As the exercise becomes comfortable, speech should gradually become more natural.STEP 5 — SUBCONSCIOUS REWIRING Speech practice is not only about the mouth and breathing. You can also train your attention by deliberately noticing successful speaking experiences and replacing old negative expectations with new, realistic experiences. Daily process 1. Complete the breathing, vocal, mirror, and reading exercises (daily). 2. Speak with a friend or family member in a normal, relaxed conversation (during the day). 3. Do not obsessively monitor every word. Focus on communicating your message. 4. If you notice a conversation in which you spoke comfortably or experienced less stammering, write it down in your journal. 5. Record what happened: Who did I speak with? What did I talk about? What felt easier? Which body part stayed relaxed? 6. Read your successful moments every night before sleeping. Subconscious thought framework Use this framework as a reminder: "I am eliminating the hold that my past bad speaking experiences have had on me. I am creating new, positive speaking experiences by learning and practicing a correct, structured path. Every successful conversation gives my mind a new experience to learn from. My body stays relaxed. My voice is calm. I do not need to erase my past; I can learn from it and build a new pattern through consistent practice." Journal example "Today I spoke with my boss about a project. I noticed my shoulders started to tense up, but I took a breath and relaxed them. I spoke clearly and confidently. The conversation went well. My jaw stayed relaxed the entire time." The goal of the journal is to collect real experiences—not to pretend that every conversation will be perfectly fluent.STEP 5B — PRACTICING WITH FAMILY AND TRUSTED FRIENDS Real-world practice with supportive people is essential for overcoming severe stammering. Regular conversation with family and closest friends creates a safe environment where you can apply the techniques you are learning without fear of judgment or mockery. Why practice with trusted people matters Practicing with family and closest friends who won't judge you or mock you provides: • A safe, judgment-free zone for speaking practice • Real conversations to apply breathing and relaxation techniques • Emotional support during the 1.5-year journey • Natural opportunities to build confidence gradually • Feedback from people who know you well and care about your progress • Reduced anxiety because you know they accept you as you are How to practice with family and friends 1. Identify your safe people: Choose family members or closest friends who are supportive and understanding. Have a conversation with them about your stammering journey. Explain that you are working to improve and would like their support as practice partners. 2. Practice regular conversations: Engage in normal, everyday conversations with these people—meals, casual chats, phone calls, or planned discussions. Use these as opportunities to apply your breathing and relaxation techniques in real situations. 3. Do not obsessively monitor: During conversations, focus on the message you want to communicate, not on monitoring every word. The goal is natural speaking, not perfect fluency. 4. Inform them of your techniques: Let them know about your breathing exercises and vocal practices. If you stammer, you can pause, take a breath, and continue. Most supportive people will be patient and understanding. 5. Request honest but kind feedback: Ask them to notice when your body is relaxed versus tense. Ask what conversations felt more fluent. This external feedback helps you build awareness. 6. Celebrate small victories: After conversations where you felt calmer or spoke more smoothly, acknowledge this progress with your trusted people. Their recognition reinforces your confidence. What to avoid • Do not practice primarily with people who mock or judge you. • Do not force yourself into large group settings before you are ready. • Do not view stammering moments as failures—they are learning opportunities.• Do not apologize repeatedly for stammering. Simple pauses and breathing are normal. Real-world practice examples Example 1: During dinner with your family, you use your breathing technique before speaking about your day. Your family notices you sound calmer. You record this in your journal. Example 2: You call a close friend. You practice speaking slowly and stretching vowels naturally. Your friend is patient and engaged. You notice your shoulders stayed relaxed throughout the call. Example 3: You practice introducing yourself to a family member's visiting friend. Using your mirror practice insights, you consciously keep your jaw relaxed. The introduction feels smoother.STEP 6 — NIGHT-TIME POSITIVE FRAMEWORK Before sleeping, read your journal and repeat a supportive statement. Keep the statement realistic and focused on your actions and progress. Choose one or more: • "I am doing my part to improve my speech and body control, one step at a time." • "I am practicing consistently (once daily) and becoming more comfortable with speaking." • "I do not need to rush my words. My body is relaxed. I communicate calmly." • "I am learning a better path to speaking and I am practicing it every day." • "My body is learning to relax when I speak. My tension is decreasing. My confidence is increasing." • "My past experiences do not have to determine every future conversation. I am creating new experiences through practice." • "The people who love me accept me for who I am. I practice with those who support me." • "Every conversation with my trusted friends builds new neural pathways for confident, calm speaking." Night routine: Read your journal → breathe comfortably → repeat your chosen framework → sleep.STEP 7 — THE 1.5-YEAR COMMITMENT ROADMAP Months 1–3: Foundation & Body Awareness • Breathing becomes automatic • Vocal exercises feel less awkward • Mirror practice reveals body tension patterns • You identify where you hold stress (jaw, shoulders, neck) • Initial practice with family members begins • Goal: Understand your physical stammer pattern and build safe practice partnerships Months 4–6: Vocal Strength & Relaxation • Speech feels smoother when reading aloud • Vocal exercises strengthen your voice • Mirror practice shows decreasing tension • You can consciously relax tense areas • Conversations with trusted friends feel more natural • Goal: Body-relaxed speech becomes easier; real-world confidence grows Months 7–9: Real-World Integration • Noticeable reduction in stammering (30–40%) • Comfortable speaking in small group conversations with family/friends • Body tension has significantly decreased • Less anxiety before speaking with trusted people • You seek more opportunities to practice with supportive friends • Goal: Stammering is not dominating interactions with those you trust Months 10–15: Expanded Confidence & Fluency Growth • Stammering reduction continues (40–55%) • Speaking with family and friends feels natural and comfortable • You notice increased fluency in everyday conversations • Anxiety about being judged decreases significantly • You may gradually venture into slightly larger social circles with careful pacing • Goal: Build momentum toward fluency; strengthen new speaking habits Months 16–18: Permanence & Confidence • Stammering is significantly reduced (50–70%) • Confidence in speaking situations with trusted people is solid • Your body stays naturally relaxed when speaking • Mirror practice shows smooth, tension-free speech • Family and friends notice lasting improvements • New speaking habits feel automatic and permanent • Goal: Changes feel permanent and automatic; celebrate your 1.5-year transformationWhat to expect: Progress is not linear. Some days will be easier than others. Stress may temporarily increase stammering. Use breathing exercises and practice with trusted friends. By month 6, you will see significant body-relaxation improvement. By month 9, real-world speaking improvements become noticeable to others. By month 18, the changes are yours to keep. Stammering is substantially reduced. Support from family and friends accelerates your progress. Keep going. The 1.5-year consistent investment (once daily) plus practice with loved ones pays massive dividends for life.STEP 8 — RECOMMENDED VIDEOS These resources are included as supplementary learning. Watch them to learn communication, confidence, mindset, body language, and the lived experience of stammering. They are not substitutes for individualized speech therapy. Speaking & confidence 1. Julian Treasure — "How to Speak So That People Want to Listen" Covers vocal exercises, powerful speaking, body language, and empathy. 2. Amy Cuddy — "Your Body Language May Shape Who You Are" Explores how posture and body language affect confidence and success. 3. Celeste Headlee — "10 Ways to Have a Better Conversation" Practical principles for authentic conversations and connection. 4. Manish Upadhyay — "A Journey From Stutter to Trainer" Personal experience of overcoming stammering and building confidence. 5. LeRon Barton — "How I Overcame My Fear of Stuttering" Focuses on fear elimination and speaking with confidence. Mind training & mental practice 6. Andy Puddicombe — "All It Takes Is 10 Mindful Minutes" Short mindfulness practice for awareness and body connection. 7. TED playlist — "Best TED Talks on Mindset and Positive Thinking" Covers mindfulness, stress, resilience, and mental habits. 8. Dr. Jeetendra Adhia — "Unleash the Power of Subconscious Mind" Explores how to reprogram subconscious patterns. Suggested viewing order: Julian Treasure → Amy Cuddy → Manish Upadhyay → LeRon Barton → Celeste Headlee → Andy Puddicombe. Final reminder The objective is not to demand perfect fluency. Build a calmer, more relaxed relationship with speaking. Practice consistently (once daily). Practice with family and closest friends who support you and won't judge you or mock you. Notice real improvements in your body and voice. Continue communicating even when speech is not perfect. Your body and your loved ones will thank you.